published in 1889; distinguish composition from publication.","creators":["Friedrich Nietzsche"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Twilight%20of%20the%20Idols","sourceSheets":[3643],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["central"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-bb64e03be9c8","title":"Wheatfield with Crows","lane":"art","start":1890,"end":1890,"approx":false,"note":"Painted at Auvers; it is not securely established as his final painting.","creators":["Vincent van Gogh"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Wheatfield%20with%20Crows","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["france","low"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=d5c48a2b7e9b944b03e04af8.webp","credit":"Vincent van Gogh","license":"Public domain","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheatfield_with_Crows","scope":"Exact title and artist matched to existing catalogue"},"mediaType78":"visual-artwork"},{"id":"t68-1e2956f97fc9","title":"Wounded Knee massacre","lane":"history","start":1890,"end":1890,"approx":false,"note":"US troops killed Lakota people; retain the specific event rather than presenting indigenous resistance as simply ending.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Wounded%20Knee%20massacre","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-9d95045cb17c","title":"Wainwright Building","lane":"architecture","start":1890,"end":1891,"approx":true,"note":"A steel-framed office building whose composition articulates tall-building function.","creators":["Louis Sullivan","Dankmar Adler"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Wainwright%20Building","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"supplemental context","dateType":"period","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=80841b782858da5c0fc636d3.webp","credit":"w_lemay","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wainwright_Building,_7th_Street_and_Chestnut_Street,_St._Louis,_MO_-_53051647915.jpg","scope":"Photograph of the identified subject or its surviving remains; not a reconstruction of its historical appearance."}},{"id":"t68-ca0cc3d6be06","title":"Mont Sainte-Victoire: series","lane":"art","start":1890,"end":1895,"approx":true,"note":"Cézanne returned to this subject across many years; the range is a study window, not the date of every version.","creators":["Paul Cézanne"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Mont%20Sainte-Victoire%3A%20series","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"supplemental work for a creator on the source sheet","dateType":"period","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=c2b58b0fd765a4a1eb0cfee7.webp","credit":"Paul Cézanne","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_C%C3%A9zanne_-_Montagne_Saint-victoire_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","scope":"Representative work from the series/cycle; the timeline range covers the series, not only this image."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-75f46fc7fd5f","title":"The Card Players: series","lane":"art","start":1890,"end":1895,"approx":true,"note":"A series generally dated to the early 1890s; the photo does not securely identify one version.","creators":["Paul Cézanne"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Card%20Players%3A%20series","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=c0c9bd517d30a5f1ad317da7.webp","credit":"Paul Cézanne","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Les_Joueurs_de_cartes%2C_par_Paul_C%C3%A9zanne.jpg","scope":"One version of Cézanne's Card Players series. The timeline spans the series; the photographed canvas is identified in the image source."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-4995b1958c6a","title":"Art Nouveau","lane":"architecture","start":1890,"end":1914,"approx":true,"note":"Approximate international movement joining architecture and decorative arts; local names and forms differ.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Art%20Nouveau","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["shared"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t69-0b060caf201a","title":"Art Nouveau, Liberty and Jugendstil","lane":"architecture","start":1890,"end":1914,"approx":true,"note":"Related international movements used regionally different names and forms. Italian Liberty and German Jugendstil belong to this context; Art Deco is a later, distinct label.","creators":[],"reference":"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/art-nouveau-an-international-style","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["france","italy","central"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept"},{"id":"t69-e3c500479154","title":"Gesamtkunstwerk and the designed interior","lane":"architecture","start":1890,"end":1914,"approx":true,"note":"The idea of a total work of art coordinates architecture, furnishings and other arts. It is not confined to a single movement or designer.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Gesamtkunstwerk%20and%20the%20designed%20interior","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["central"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept"},{"id":"t68-5bb2363ee7e4","title":"Naum Gabo","lane":"people","start":1890,"end":1977,"approx":false,"note":"Constructivist sculptor investigating space, transparency and movement.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Naum%20Gabo","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-c23f64d9e374","title":"Max Ernst","lane":"people","start":1891,"end":1976,"approx":false,"note":"Dada and Surrealist artist employing collage and experimental techniques.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Max%20Ernst","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["central","france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-6171d8820364","title":"Rouen Cathedral: series","lane":"art","start":1892,"end":1894,"approx":true,"note":"Serial painting compares changing light on one architectural motif; production and exhibition dates differ.","creators":["Claude Monet"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Rouen%20Cathedral%3A%20series","sourceSheets":[3643],"sourceMapping":"supplemental work for a creator on the source sheet","dateType":"period","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","museumAssetId78":"PDFART48-157","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=309bb221c9e9fc1947ec8b8e.webp","credit":"Claude Monet","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RouenCathedral_Monet_1894.jpg","scope":"Representative work from the series/cycle; the timeline range covers the series, not only this image. Shown: Rouen Cathedral in Full Sunlight · 1892–1893"},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-275c6dbd6a63","title":"At the Moulin Rouge","lane":"art","start":1892,"end":1895,"approx":true,"note":"A modern nightlife scene with radical cropping and unnatural lighting; commonly dated approximately 1892–1895.","creators":["Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=At%20the%20Moulin%20Rouge","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"supplemental work for a creator on the source sheet","dateType":"period","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=6511ce3d91fdc21653a19e03.webp","credit":"Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henri_de_Toulouse-Lautrec%2C_At_the_Moulin_Rouge.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-df76ee732010","title":"New Zealand grants women parliamentary voting rights","lane":"history","start":1893,"end":1893,"approx":false,"note":"A landmark in national suffrage; eligibility and participation still depended on other legal conditions.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=New%20Zealand%20grants%20women%20parliamentary%20voting%20rights","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-1c449f97fb23","title":"The Scream","lane":"art","start":1893,"end":1893,"approx":false,"note":"Several painted and printed versions exist; 1893 refers to the early painted version.","creators":["Edvard Munch"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Scream","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"supplemental work for a creator on the source sheet","dateType":"year","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=303bfcb847f3ceb56b920f06.webp","credit":"Edvard Munch","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edvard_Munch%2C_1893%2C_The_Scream%2C_oil%2C_tempera_and_pastel_on_cardboard%2C_91_x_73_cm%2C_National_Gallery_of_Norway.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-7754f17e77c9","title":"Dreyfus affair begins","lane":"history","start":1894,"end":1894,"approx":false,"note":"A wrongful treason conviction exposed antisemitism and divided French society; Zola intervened in 1898.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Dreyfus%20affair%20begins","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-e35d5bc71db8","title":"Marconi's early radio experiments","lane":"history","start":1895,"end":1895,"approx":false,"note":"Wireless communication developed through several researchers; distinguish experiments from later commercial transmission.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Marconi%27s%20early%20radio%20experiments","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-e1af717c8e67","title":"Röntgen discovers X-rays","lane":"history","start":1895,"end":1895,"approx":false,"note":"A major transformation in medical imaging and physical science.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=R%C3%B6ntgen%20discovers%20X-rays","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["central"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-f333d3951437","title":"First modern Olympic Games in Athens","lane":"history","start":1896,"end":1896,"approx":false,"note":"The revived international Games drew on an idealised classical heritage.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=First%20modern%20Olympic%20Games%20in%20Athens","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["eastmed"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-80268f20f603","title":"Where Do We Come From? What Are We? Where Are We Going?","lane":"art","start":1897,"end":1898,"approx":true,"note":"Painted in Tahiti in 1897–1898; a symbolic work shaped by colonial-era encounters and the artist's inventions.","creators":["Paul Gauguin"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Where%20Do%20We%20Come%20From%3F%20What%20Are%20We%3F%20Where%20Are%20We%20Going%3F","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["other","france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=381b37eaa8802529aa007de7.webp","credit":"Paul Gauguin","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gauguin_-_Where_Do_We_Come_From%3F_What_Are_We%3F_Where_Are_We_Going%3F_%281897-98%29.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t69-d04bdbaf24ce","title":"Vienna Secession and artistic independence","lane":"art","start":1897,"end":1914,"approx":true,"note":"The Secession challenged established exhibition structures and embraced multiple artistic approaches. Klimt, Olbrich and Hoffmann worked in different media.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Vienna%20Secession%20and%20artistic%20independence","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["central"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t68-c967e980955a","title":"Water Lilies: series","lane":"art","start":1897,"end":1926,"approx":true,"note":"A long-running Giverny subject culminating in immersive late panels; no single canvas is implied.","creators":["Claude Monet"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Water%20Lilies%3A%20series","sourceSheets":[3643],"sourceMapping":"supplemental work for a creator on the source sheet","dateType":"period","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","museumAssetId78":"M77-MOMA-80220","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=53013d234e0fc4b7976cc101.webp","credit":"Claude Monet","license":"Image © respective rights holders · MoMA collection","url":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/80220","scope":"Representative work from the series/cycle; the timeline range covers the series, not only this image. Shown: Water Lilies · 1914-26"},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-e81d4c5a0657","title":"Paul Delvaux","lane":"people","start":1897,"end":1994,"approx":false,"note":"Belgian painter of enigmatic figures, architecture and nocturnal settings.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Paul%20Delvaux","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["low"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-f09297ff6c7f","title":"Curie research identifies polonium and radium","lane":"history","start":1898,"end":1898,"approx":false,"note":"Marie and Pierre Curie's investigations advanced the study of radioactivity.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Curie%20research%20identifies%20polonium%20and%20radium","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-46522f29972d","title":"Fashoda Incident","lane":"history","start":1898,"end":1898,"approx":false,"note":"A colonial confrontation in Sudan; later Anglo-French rapprochement had multiple causes.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Fashoda%20Incident","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["britain","france","other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-cbc908209ff1","title":"Spanish–American War","lane":"history","start":1898,"end":1898,"approx":false,"note":"Spain lost control of major overseas possessions; US imperial power expanded.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Spanish%E2%80%93American%20War","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["iberia","other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-51939b46594c","title":"Zola publishes J'accuse","lane":"history","start":1898,"end":1898,"approx":false,"note":"An open letter challenged the handling of the Dreyfus affair.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Zola%20publishes%20J%27accuse","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-9d1de840e736","title":"The Large Bathers","lane":"art","start":1898,"end":1906,"approx":true,"note":"A late work combining figures and landscape into a constructed composition; developed over several years.","creators":["Paul Cézanne"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Large%20Bathers","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","museumAssetId78":"PDFART48-168","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=3abf80a911729358c23d7263.webp","credit":"Paul Cézanne","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_C%C3%A9zanne%2C_French_-_The_Large_Bathers_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","scope":"The Large Bathers, Philadelphia Museum of Art version, 1906. Full painting."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-14b7b273f8ba","title":"René Magritte","lane":"people","start":1898,"end":1967,"approx":false,"note":"Belgian Surrealist examining the relationship between images, language and objects.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Ren%C3%A9%20Magritte","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["low"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-0ed181d85b66","title":"Alexander Calder","lane":"people","start":1898,"end":1976,"approx":false,"note":"American sculptor known for mobiles and large outdoor works.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Alexander%20Calder","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-f0f56df4f81b","title":"Henry Moore","lane":"people","start":1898,"end":1986,"approx":false,"note":"Sculptor of reclining figures and organic forms, often intended for outdoor settings.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Henry%20Moore","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["britain"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-5d876b351e83","title":"First Hague Peace Conference","lane":"history","start":1899,"end":1899,"approx":false,"note":"International efforts to regulate warfare and arbitration preceded the world wars.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=First%20Hague%20Peace%20Conference","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["low","shared"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-ae69af07c982","title":"Lucio Fontana","lane":"people","start":1899,"end":1968,"approx":false,"note":"Artist associated with Spatialism; cuts and perforations turn pictorial surface into actual space.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Lucio%20Fontana","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["italy","other"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-24cfde8f07f6","title":"The Interpretation of Dreams","lane":"people","start":1900,"end":1900,"approx":false,"note":"Dated 1900 on publication, though released in late 1899; the photo's 1910 marker is not its first publication.","creators":["Sigmund Freud"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Interpretation%20of%20Dreams","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["central"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t69-b313f49d3a7e","title":"Expressionism and emotional distortion","lane":"art","start":1900,"end":1930,"approx":true,"note":"Distorted colour, form or space can communicate psychological intensity. Expressionism is not the same movement as Abstract Expressionism after World War II.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Expressionism%20and%20emotional%20distortion","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["central"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t69-1d353b9d3ec0","title":"Totalitarianism, fascism and democracy","lane":"history","start":1900,"end":1945,"approx":true,"note":"Compare institutions, repression, propaganda and political mobilisation. Fascism and Nazism share features but have distinct histories; democratic elections alone do not secure liberal democracy.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Totalitarianism%2C%20fascism%20and%20democracy","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["central","italy"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept"},{"id":"t69-b0c6842a893b","title":"Organic architecture and the site","lane":"architecture","start":1900,"end":1960,"approx":true,"note":"Wright sought integration of building, inhabitants, materials and landscape. Organic does not simply mean a curving shape or the use of plants.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Organic%20architecture%20and%20the%20site","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept"},{"id":"t68-b66b9dfc527f","title":"First Nobel Prizes awarded","lane":"history","start":1901,"end":1901,"approx":false,"note":"The awards reflected Alfred Nobel's endowment; categories and institutions have specific histories.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=First%20Nobel%20Prizes%20awarded","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["shared"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-d532f8f5ef8d","title":"Alberto Giacometti","lane":"people","start":1901,"end":1966,"approx":false,"note":"Swiss sculptor working in Paris; Surrealist objects preceded elongated postwar figures.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Alberto%20Giacometti","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["central","france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-354646554ad2","title":"Marino Marini","lane":"people","start":1901,"end":1980,"approx":false,"note":"Italian sculptor whose recurring horse-and-rider theme changes dramatically across periods.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Marino%20Marini","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-d4e10d5d9422","title":"Jean Dubuffet","lane":"people","start":1901,"end":1985,"approx":false,"note":"French artist who developed the concept of Art Brut.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Jean%20Dubuffet","sourceSheets":[3646],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-2587c48eb87e","title":"Marcel Breuer","lane":"people","start":1902,"end":1981,"approx":false,"note":"Bauhaus designer and architect, known for tubular steel furniture and later concrete buildings.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Marcel%20Breuer","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["central","other"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-5ed249a19d70","title":"Wright brothers' powered flight","lane":"history","start":1903,"end":1903,"approx":false,"note":"A sustained, controlled flight at Kitty Hawk became a milestone in aviation.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Wright%20brothers%27%20powered%20flight","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-edd10ed89ace","title":"Mark Rothko","lane":"people","start":1903,"end":1970,"approx":false,"note":"Painter associated with large fields of colour; distinct from Pollock's action-painting methods.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Mark%20Rothko","sourceSheets":[3647],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-e1462f408752","title":"Entente Cordiale","lane":"history","start":1904,"end":1904,"approx":false,"note":"Agreements settled colonial disputes; they were not yet a single automatic military alliance.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Entente%20Cordiale","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["britain","france"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-26be6fc2928c","title":"Russo-Japanese War","lane":"history","start":1904,"end":1905,"approx":false,"note":"Japanese victory challenged European assumptions of imperial superiority.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Russo-Japanese%20War","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-2bc7953405dc","title":"Salvador Dalí","lane":"people","start":1904,"end":1989,"approx":false,"note":"Spanish Surrealist known for precise rendering of dreamlike scenes.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Salvador%20Dal%C3%AD","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["iberia"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-bee4514d0828","title":"Bloody Sunday in Saint Petersburg","lane":"history","start":1905,"end":1905,"approx":false,"note":"Violence against petitioners helped ignite the Russian Revolution of 1905.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Bloody%20Sunday%20in%20Saint%20Petersburg","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-48782d69bd16","title":"Einstein publishes special relativity","lane":"history","start":1905,"end":1905,"approx":false,"note":"A new account of space and time; general relativity followed in 1915.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Einstein%20publishes%20special%20relativity","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["central"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-8bc3ff5e991e","title":"First Moroccan Crisis","lane":"history","start":1905,"end":1905,"approx":false,"note":"German intervention challenged French influence in Morocco and tested European diplomacy.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=First%20Moroccan%20Crisis","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["central","france","other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-ad68db953ef1","title":"Industrial Workers of the World founded","lane":"history","start":1905,"end":1905,"approx":false,"note":"An internationalist labour organisation founded in Chicago.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Industrial%20Workers%20of%20the%20World%20founded","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-a16a8a468cf8","title":"Woman with a Hat","lane":"art","start":1905,"end":1905,"approx":false,"note":"Non-naturalistic colour helped define the public reception of Fauvism.","creators":["Henri Matisse"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Woman%20with%20a%20Hat","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=1b64d606b5be1a3f9ccd74ac.webp","credit":"Henri Matisse","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matisse-Woman-with-a-Hat.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-635658e41984","title":"The Joy of Life","lane":"art","start":1905,"end":1906,"approx":true,"note":"Also called Le Bonheur de vivre. Fauvist colour and an idealised figure landscape; the handwritten placement is not a precise creation date.","creators":["Henri Matisse"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Bonheur_de_vivre","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=0d926e59a9f99f2dd975a42b.webp","credit":"Henri Matisse","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bonheur_Matisse.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-7dd072993daf","title":"Fauvism","lane":"art","start":1905,"end":1908,"approx":true,"note":"Expressive, non-naturalistic colour; dates mark a short collective moment rather than all later work by these artists.","creators":["Henri Matisse","André Derain"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Fauvism","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t69-b0fa09c72c10","title":"Fauvist colour versus naturalistic colour","lane":"art","start":1905,"end":1910,"approx":true,"note":"Fauvist colour can be organised for expressive and compositional reasons rather than reproducing local colour. Strong colour alone does not identify a specific artist.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Fauvist%20colour%20versus%20naturalistic%20colour","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t68-02ede2d88618","title":"Seated Woman","lane":"art","start":1906,"end":1920,"approx":true,"note":"Unresolved title: several Modigliani portraits have this generic name. Broad working period only; no specific canvas or exact date is asserted.","creators":["Amedeo Modigliani"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Seated%20Woman","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"contextual identification","dateType":"period","threads67":["france","italy"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","dateQualifier":"Source title / version unresolved","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=485baaa5d9f9ac0a6899abb6.webp","credit":"Amedeo Modigliani","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amedeo_Modigliani%2C_Seated_Woman_in_a_Blue_Dress_%281917-1919%29.jpg","scope":"Contextual example: Seated Woman in a Blue Dress, 1917–1919. The handwritten generic title does not identify a unique canvas; this image is not claimed as its exact identification."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-df23c72edbcb","title":"Les Demoiselles d'Avignon","lane":"art","start":1907,"end":1907,"approx":false,"note":"A radical reworking of the figure and pictorial space that preceded mature Cubism.","creators":["Pablo Picasso"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Les%20Demoiselles%20d%27Avignon","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["france","iberia"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=f69a37b30649d071be0d6636.webp","credit":"Image reference from Wikipedia","license":"Wikipedia non-free reference · reuse not cleared","url":"https://www.moma.org/explore/inside_out/2010/04/14/moma-offsite-where-did-it-go/","scope":"Exact title and artist matched to existing catalogue"},"mediaType78":"visual-artwork"},{"id":"t68-57cb0c406c3c","title":"Triple Entente diplomatic alignment","lane":"history","start":1907,"end":1907,"approx":false,"note":"The Anglo-Russian Convention completed an alignment with France; distinguish from the Triple Alliance.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Triple%20Entente%20diplomatic%20alignment","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["britain","france","other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-7a586eab8403","title":"The Kiss","lane":"art","start":1907,"end":1908,"approx":true,"note":"Gold and decorative pattern envelop the embracing figures; a key work of Klimt's golden period.","creators":["Gustav Klimt"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Kiss","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"supplemental work for a creator on the source sheet","dateType":"period","threads67":["central"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=a6d3c1b825d7a0751b5ea169.webp","credit":"Gustav Klimt","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Kiss_-_Gustav_Klimt_-_Google_Cultural_Institute.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t69-01747b46cc83","title":"Analytic and Synthetic Cubism","lane":"art","start":1907,"end":1914,"approx":true,"note":"Analytic Cubism fragments and examines forms; Synthetic Cubism introduces more constructed signs, collage and broader shapes. These are phases, not two unrelated schools.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Analytic%20and%20Synthetic%20Cubism","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t68-1b1342eae4cf","title":"Cubism","lane":"art","start":1907,"end":1914,"approx":true,"note":"Analytic and Synthetic phases rethought representation, viewpoint and pictorial construction.","creators":["Pablo Picasso","Georges Braque"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Cubism","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"period","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t68-d76b5f61a760","title":"Austria-Hungary annexes Bosnia-Herzegovina","lane":"history","start":1908,"end":1908,"approx":false,"note":"Annexation intensified Balkan and great-power tensions.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Austria-Hungary%20annexes%20Bosnia-Herzegovina","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["central","eastmed"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-f49247b19579","title":"Harmony in Red","lane":"art","start":1908,"end":1908,"approx":false,"note":"Also called The Red Room; colour and decorative pattern flatten spatial depth.","creators":["Henri Matisse"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Harmony%20in%20Red","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=0624155f0369b426a47bf7f9.webp","credit":"Henri Matisse","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matisse-The-Dessert-Harmony-in-Red-Henri-1908-fast.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-87e416e34b74","title":"Houses at L'Estaque","lane":"art","start":1908,"end":1908,"approx":false,"note":"Geometric simplification of landscape is a key step towards Cubism.","creators":["Georges Braque"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Houses%20at%20L%27Estaque","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=78b94d1efbe3b9e3cd1bcdf3.webp","credit":"Georges Braque","license":"Copyrighted artwork; see source reproduction rights","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Georges_Braque,_1908,_Maisons_à_l'Estaque_(Houses_at_L'Estaque),_oil_on_canvas,_73_x_59.5_cm,_Kunst_Museum_Bern.jpg","scope":"Houses at L’Estaque, 1908, Kunstmuseum Bern. This is the Bern canvas, not the related Houses and Tree at LaM."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-3df53c1a3f04","title":"Willem de Kooning","lane":"people","start":1908,"end":1997,"approx":false,"note":"Dutch-born Abstract Expressionist active in New York; figuration and abstraction interact.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Willem%20de%20Kooning","sourceSheets":[3647],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["low","other"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-58d6eed54a1d","title":"AEG Turbine Factory","lane":"architecture","start":1909,"end":1909,"approx":false,"note":"Industrial architecture and corporate design intersect in Berlin.","creators":["Peter Behrens"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=AEG%20Turbine%20Factory","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"supplemental context","dateType":"year","threads67":["central"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=bcad7bb57c2a2d8ca225e852.webp","credit":"Doris Antony, Berlin","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlin_AEG_Turbinenfabrik.jpg","scope":"Photograph of the identified subject or its surviving remains; not a reconstruction of its historical appearance."}},{"id":"t68-12c8b4f5e382","title":"Futurist Manifesto published","lane":"art","start":1909,"end":1909,"approx":false,"note":"The manifesto appeared in Le Figaro; Futurist painting and architecture manifestos followed.","creators":["Filippo Tommaso Marinetti"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manifesto_of_Futurism","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["italy","france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t69-d3188a57e846","title":"Futurism: speed, simultaneity and modern life","lane":"art","start":1909,"end":1930,"approx":true,"note":"Futurists explored movement through repeated or interpenetrating forms. Their celebration of machines and war requires historical context, not uncritical endorsement.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Futurism","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t68-f305751c9b61","title":"Dance","lane":"art","start":1910,"end":1910,"approx":false,"note":"The large 1910 version emphasises rhythm, simplified bodies and strong colour; an earlier version exists.","creators":["Henri Matisse"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Dance","sourceSheets":[3644],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["france"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","museumAssetId78":"PDFART48-185","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=f96a3d75d2958ca54aa4a7eb.webp","credit":"Henri Matisse","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matissedance.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies. Shown: Dance (Hermitage, 1910) · 1910"},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-ddba969b1961","title":"Mexican Revolution begins","lane":"history","start":1910,"end":1910,"approx":false,"note":"A prolonged revolution involving different movements, leaders and competing social programmes.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Mexican%20Revolution%20begins","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t69-e83acf02b8a7","title":"Abstraction versus non-objective art","lane":"art","start":1910,"end":1940,"approx":true,"note":"Abstraction may transform a recognisable subject; non-objective art need not start from one. Do not use abstract as a synonym for every modern artwork.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Abstraction%20versus%20non-objective%20art","sourceSheets":[],"sourceMapping":"Editorial study concept, not a photo transcription","dateType":"study window","dateQualifier":"Study period, not an event date","threads67":["shared"],"setting67":"Historical / cultural study context","kind69":"concept","mediaType78":"context"},{"id":"t68-c8f1ada187ec","title":"Dynamism of a Dog on a Leash","lane":"art","start":1912,"end":1912,"approx":false,"note":"Repeated forms suggest motion and the influence of photographic studies.","creators":["Giacomo Balla"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Dynamism%20of%20a%20Dog%20on%20a%20Leash","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=94dbd45784b54737e525963c.webp","credit":"Giacomo Balla","license":"Copyrighted artwork; reproduction from Wikipedia. Commercial-use permission not established.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Giacomo_Balla%2C_1912%2C_Dynamism_of_a_Dog_on_a_Leash%2C_oil_on_canvas%2C_89.8_x_109.8_cm%2C_Albright-Knox_Art_Gallery.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-0f6702c28a37","title":"French protectorate established in Morocco","lane":"history","start":1912,"end":1912,"approx":false,"note":"The Treaty of Fez formalised the protectorate; the source's 1909 marker is not the treaty date.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=French%20protectorate%20established%20in%20Morocco","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["france","other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-e98200a2e216","title":"Titanic sinks","lane":"history","start":1912,"end":1912,"approx":false,"note":"The passenger liner sank on its maiden voyage after striking an iceberg.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Titanic%20sinks","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["britain","other"],"setting67":"Historical setting","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-60f4050324b8","title":"Jackson Pollock","lane":"people","start":1912,"end":1956,"approx":false,"note":"Abstract Expressionist painter known for poured and dripped compositions.","creators":[],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Jackson%20Pollock","sourceSheets":[3646],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"lifespan","threads67":["other"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16"},{"id":"t68-acc8cc2ed3af","title":"Composition VII","lane":"art","start":1913,"end":1913,"approx":true,"note":"An abstract composition; the sheet says Composition without a number, so this is contextual rather than an exact identification.","creators":["Wassily Kandinsky"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Composition%20VII","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"contextual identification","dateType":"year","threads67":["central"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","dateQualifier":"Source title / version unresolved","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","museumAssetId78":"PDFART48-194","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=4dee69a6f481824acd48c495.webp","credit":"Wassily Kandinsky","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Composition_VII_-_Wassily_Kandinsky,_GAC.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies. Shown: Composition VII · 1913"},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-8f7ccc2d89be","title":"Dynamism of a Cyclist","lane":"art","start":1913,"end":1913,"approx":false,"note":"Futurist painting explores movement rather than depicting a static anatomical study.","creators":["Umberto Boccioni"],"reference":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Dynamism%20of%20a%20Cyclist","sourceSheets":[3645],"sourceMapping":"item","dateType":"year","threads67":["italy"],"setting67":"Cultural / geographical association; see work context","sourceBatch68":"Sheets 11–16","mediaType78":"visual-artwork","image":{"src":"/.netlify/functions/access?action=media&name=4c063c9346a211f62d2d9438.webp","credit":"Umberto Boccioni","license":"Public domain","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umberto_Boccioni%2C_1913%2C_Dynamism_of_a_Cyclist_%28Dinamismo_di_un_ciclista%29%2C_oil_on_canvas%2C_70_x_95_cm%2C_Gianni_Mattioli_Collection%2C_on_long-term_loan_to_the_Peggy_Guggenheim_Collection%2C_Venice.jpg","scope":"Identified work. Sculpture photographs may show later casts or surviving copies."},"imageChecked78":"2026-10-05"},{"id":"t68-054b0dcf090f","title":"Unique Forms of Continuity in Space","lane":"art","start":1913,"end":1913,"approx":false,"no